{"task_id": "HumanEval/109", "solution": "def move_one_ball(arr: list[int]) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"\n    Checks if an array can be sorted into non-decreasing order by repeatedly applying a right-shift operation.\n    It generates all possible shifts and compares each with the sorted version of the array.\n    \"\"\"\n    n = len(arr)\n\n    # FR2: If the input array is empty, it is considered sorted.\n    if n == 0:\n        return True\n\n    # Generate the target sorted version of the array.\n    sorted_arr = sorted(arr)\n    \n    # Create a mutable copy of the input array to perform shift operations on.\n    current_arr = list(arr) \n\n    # Iterate 'n' times, covering all unique right-shifted versions of the array.\n    for _ in range(n):\n        # FR1: If the current array matches the sorted version, it is sortable.\n        if current_arr == sorted_arr:\n            return True\n        \n        # Perform one right shift operation as per BR1.\n        # The last element is moved to the first position.\n        last_element = current_arr.pop()\n        current_arr.insert(0, last_element)\n            \n    # If no shifted version matched the sorted array after checking all possibilities, return False.\n    return False"}
