{"task_id": "HumanEval/155", "solution": "def even_odd_count(num):\n    \"\"\"Given an integer, return a tuple that has the number of even and odd digits respectively.\"\"\"\n    \n    if not isinstance(num, int):\n        raise TypeError(\"Input must be an integer.\")\n\n    num_str = str(abs(num)) # Converts the number to its absolute string form for digit iteration.\n    even_count = 0\n    odd_count = 0\n\n    for digit_char in num_str:\n        digit = int(digit_char)\n        if digit % 2 == 0:\n            even_count += 1\n        else:\n            odd_count += 1\n            \n    return even_count, odd_count"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/159", "solution": "def eat(number, need, remaining):\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates the total carrots eaten and the number of carrots left after a rabbit's meal.\n    The rabbit eats either its need or all remaining carrots, whichever is less.\n    \"\"\"\n    carrots_eaten_this_meal = min(need, remaining)\n    total_eaten = number + carrots_eaten_this_meal\n    carrots_left = remaining - carrots_eaten_this_meal\n    return [total_eaten, carrots_left]"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/157", "solution": "def right_angle_triangle(a, b, c):\n    \"\"\"\n    Given the lengths of the three sides of a triangle. Return True if the three\n    sides form a right-angled triangle, False otherwise.\n    A right-angled triangle is a triangle in which one angle is right angle or \n    90 degree.\n    Example:\n    right_angle_triangle(3, 4, 5) == True\n    right_angle_triangle(1, 2, 3) == False\n    \"\"\"\n    # Validate side lengths as numeric and positive.\n    if not all(isinstance(s, (int, float)) for s in [a, b, c]):\n        raise TypeError(\"Side lengths must be numeric.\")\n    if not all(s > 0 for s in [a, b, c]):\n        raise ValueError(\"Side lengths must be positive.\")\n\n    # Define a small tolerance for floating-point comparisons.\n    EPSILON = 1e-9\n\n    # Calculate squares of the sides and sort them to find the potential hypotenuse.\n    sides_squared = sorted([a**2, b**2, c**2])\n    \n    # Check if the sum of the squares of the two shorter sides is approximately\n    # equal to the square of the longest side (hypotenuse).\n    return abs(sides_squared[0] + sides_squared[1] - sides_squared[2]) < EPSILON"}
